or Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies (NAACL-HLT).\nL289:   * Ni et al. (2019) Jianmo Ni, Jiacheng Li, and Julian McAuley. 2019. Justifying recommendations using distantly-labeled reviews and fine-grained aspects. In Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP).\nL290:   * Reimers and Gurevych (2019) Nils Reimers and Iryna Gurevych. 2019. Sentence-bert: Sentence embeddings using siamese bert-networks. In Proceedings of the 2019 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing and the 9th International Joint Conference on Natural Language Processing (EMNLP-IJCNLP).\nL291:   * Richardson et al. (2023) Chris Richardson, Yao Zhang, Kellen Gillespie, Sudipta Kar, Arshdeep Singh, Zeynab Raeesy, Omar Zia Khan, and Abhinav Sethy. 2023. Integrating summarization and retrieval for enhanced personalization via large language models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.20081.\nL292:   * Robertson et al. (2004) Stephen Robertson, Hugo Zaragoza, and Michael Taylor. 2004. Simple bm25 extension to multiple weighted fields. In Proceedings of the thirteenth ACM international conference on Information and knowledge management.\nL293:   * Rolnick et al. (2019) David Rolnick, Arun Ahuja, Jonathan Schwarz, Timothy Lillicrap, and Gregory Wayne. 2019. Experience replay for continual learning. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS).\nL294:   * Santurkar et al. (2023) Shibani Santurkar, Esin Durmus, Faisal Ladhak, Cinoo Lee, Percy Liang, and Tatsunori Hashimoto. 2023. Whose opinions do language models reflect? In Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML).\nL295:   * Seo et al. (2025) Minhyuk Seo, Hyunseo Koh, and Jonghyun Choi. 2025. Budgeted online continual learning by adaptive layer freezing and frequency-based sampling. In International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR).\nL296:   * Shi et al. (2024) Haizhou Shi, Zihao Xu, Hengyi Wang, Weiyi Qin, Wenyuan Wang, Yibin Wang, Zifeng Wang, Sayna Ebrahimi, and Hao Wang. 2024. Continual learning of large language models: A comprehensive survey. ACM Computing Surveys.\nL297:   * Tan et al. (2024) Zhaoxuan Tan, Qingkai Zeng, Yijun Tian, Zheyuan Liu, Bing Yin, and Meng Jiang. 2024. Democratizing large language models via personalized parameter-efficient fine-tuning. In Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP).\nL298:   * Team et al. (2025) Gemma Team, Aishwarya Kamath, Johan Ferret, Shreya Pathak, Nino Vieillard, Ramona Merhej, Sarah Perrin, Tatiana Matejovicova, Alexandre Ramé, Morgane Rivière, and 1 others. 2025. Gemma 3 technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.19786.\nL299:   * Wang et al. (2023) Xiao Wang, Tianze Chen, Qiming Ge, Han Xia, Rong Bao, Rui Zheng, Qi Zhang, Tao Gui, and Xuan-Jing Huang. 2023. Orthogonal subspace learning for language model continual learning. In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2023.\nL300:   * Xiang et al. (2023) Jiannan Xiang, Tianhua Tao, Yi Gu, Tianmin Shu, Zirui Wang, Zichao Yang, and Zhiting Hu. 2023. Language models meet world models: Embodied experiences enhance language models. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS).\nL301:   * Yang et al. (2025) An Yang, Anfeng Li, Baosong Yang, Beichen Zhang, Binyuan Hui, Bo Zheng, Bowen Yu, Chang Gao, Chengen Huang, Chenxu Lv, and 1 others. 2025. Qwen3 technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2505.09388.\nL302:   * Zhang et al. (2025) Linhai Zhang, Jialong Wu, Deyu Zhou, and Yulan He. 2025. Proper: A progressive learning framework for personalized large language models with group-level adaptation. In Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL).\nL303:   * Zhang et al. (2024) Zhehao Zhang, Ryan A Rossi, Branislav Kveton, Yijia Shao, Diyi Yang, Hamed Zamani, Franck Dernoncourt, Joe Barrow, Tong Yu, Sungchul Kim, and 1 others. 2024. Personalization of large language models: A survey. arXiv preprint arXiv:2411.00027.\nL304: ## Appendix A Implementation Details\nL305: ### A.1 Training and inference algorithms\nL306: \nL307: Algorithm 1 SPRInG training algorithm\nL308: \nL309: Input: Base LLM $\\mathcal{M}_{\\theta}$ (frozen), initial user adapter $\\phi_{u}^{(0)}$, interaction stream $\\{\\mathcal{D}_{u}^{(t)}\\}_{t\\geq 1}$, scoring function $\\mathcal{S}(\\cdot)$ (Eq. cite85†1 ), top percentile $p$, buffer budget $N_{\\max}$\nL310: \nL311: $\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(0)}\\leftarrow\\emptyset$\nL312: \nL313: for $t=1,2,\\cdots$ do\nL314: \nL315:    /* Drift sample selection */\nL316: \nL317:   for $(q,a)\\in\\mathcal{D}_{u}^{(t)}$ do\nL318:    $\\mathcal{S}_{t}(q,a)\\leftarrow\\mathcal{S}(q,a;\\mathcal{M}_{\\theta},\\phi_{u}^{(t-1)})$\nL319: \nL320:   end for\nL321: \nL322:   $\\mathcal{D}_{\\texttt{drift}}^{(t)}\\leftarrow\\{(q,a)\\in\\mathcal{D}_{u}^{(t)}~|~\\mathcal{S}_{t}(q,a)\\geq\\tau_{p}\\}$\nL323: \nL324:    /* Parametric adapter update */\nL325: \nL326:   $\\phi_{u}^{(t)}\\leftarrow\\phi_{u}^{(t-1)}\\oplus\\mathcal{D}_{\\texttt{drift}}^{(t)}$\nL327: \nL328:    /* Replay buffer construction */\nL329: \nL330:   $\\mathcal{C}_{u}^{(t)}\\leftarrow\\mathcal{D}_{\\texttt{drift}}^{(t)}\\cup\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t-1)}$\nL331:   for $(q,a)\\in\\mathcal{C}_{u}^{(t)}$ do\nL332: \nL333:    $\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime}_{t}(q,a)\\leftarrow\\mathcal{S}(q,a;\\mathcal{M}_{\\theta},\\phi_{u}^{(t)})$\nL334: \nL335:   end for\nL336: \nL337:   $\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}\\leftarrow\\operatorname*{Top-N_{\\max}}_{(q,a)\\in\\mathcal{C}_{u}^{(t)}}\\left(\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime}_{t}(q,a)\\right)$\nL338: \nL339: end for\nL340: \nL341: return period-wise adapters $\\{\\phi_{u}^{(t)}\\}$ and replay buffers $\\{\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}\\}$\nL342: Algorithm cite74†1 presents the training pipeline for SPRInG. It identifies drift samples based on the scoring function (Eq. cite85†1 ), updates both the parametric adapter, and the replay buffer within the budget $N_{\\max}$.\nL343: \nL344: Algorithm 2 SPRInG inference algorithm\nL345: \nL346: Input: User-adapted model $\\mathcal{M}_{u}^{(t)}$, replay buffer $\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}$, test query $q$, retrieval top-$k$, relevance threshold $\\sigma$, interpolation weight $\\lambda$\nL347: \nL348: /* Relevance gated retrieval */\nL349: $\\mathcal{R}_{q}\\leftarrow\\text{Top-}k\\{h\\in\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}\\mid\\textsc{Sim}(q,h)\\}$\nL350: \nL351: $\\mathbf{c}_{\\texttt{ret}}\\leftarrow\\textsc{Concat}\\{h\\in\\mathcal{R}_{q}\\mid\\textsc{Sim}(q,h)>\\sigma\\}$\nL352: \nL353: /* Logit interpolation */\nL354: \nL355: $a_{<1}\\leftarrow\\emptyset$\nL356: \nL357: for $j=1,2,\\cdots$ until EOS do\nL358: \nL359:   Compute $p_{\\texttt{vanilla}}$ and $p_{\\texttt{RAG}}$ (Eqs. cite97†6 , cite98†7 )\nL360: \nL361:   Compute $P_{\\texttt{final}}$ by interpolation (Eq. cite87†8 )\nL362: \nL363:   $\\hat{a}_{j}\\sim P_{\\texttt{final}}(\\cdot)$\nL364:   $a_{<j+1}\\leftarrow a_{<j}\\oplus\\hat{a}_{j}$\nL365: \nL366: end for\nL367: \nL368: return generated answer $a$\nL369: \nL370: Algorithm cite77†2 details the inference process. It incorporates the relevance-gated retrieval and the logit interpolation mechanism (Eq. cite87†8 ) to generate the final response.\nL371: ### A.2 Replay buffer retention policies\nL372: To effectively manage the limited memory of the replay buffer, we investigate four distinct retention policies. For strategies requiring semantic awareness, we generate embeddings using Sentence-BERT (cite99†Reimers and Gurevych, 2019 ) with the all-mpnet-base-v2, which provides a robust balance between encoding speed and semantic accuracy.\nL373: We then group samples into $K$ clusters using the $K$-Means algorithm, where the optimal $K$ is determined dynamically by maximizing the Silhouette Score within the range $[2,10]$.\nL374:   * $\\circ$\nL375: \nL376: Global Highest: Prioritizes absolute difficulty. We sort all candidates in $\\mathcal{C}_{u}^{(t)}$ by their scores $\\mathcal{S^{\\prime}}$ and retain the top-$N_{\\max}$ samples.\nL377: \nL378:   * $\\circ$\nL379: \nL380: Cluster-wise: Prioritizes \"hard topics.\" We calculate the average $\\mathcal{S^{\\prime}}$ score for each cluster and fill the buffer by selecting entire clusters with the highest averages. If the last selected cluster exceeds the remaining capacity, we select samples within it by highest score.\nL381:   * $\\circ$\nL382: \nL383: Cluster Round: Balances diversity with difficulty. We iteratively select the single hardest sample (highest $\\mathcal{S^{\\prime}}$) from each cluster in a round-robin fashion until the capacity $N_{\\max}$ is reached. This ensures a uniform representation of all semantic groups (hard and soft topics alike).\nL384: \nL385:   * $\\circ$\nL386: \nL387: Random: A baseline strategy that uniformly samples $N_{\\max}$ interactions from the candidate pool without regard to difficulty or semantics.\nL388: Table 4: Ablation on replay buffer retention policies. Comparison of different strategies for selecting interactions to be retained in the memory buffer under a fixed budget $N_{\\max}$.\nL389: Methods  | Abstract Generation  | Review Writing\nL390: --- | --- | ---\nL391: ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L\nL392: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL393: Global highest (Ours)  | 0.350  | 0.189  | 0.332  | 0.145\nL394: Cluster round  | 0.346  | 0.188  | 0.331  | 0.145\nL395: Cluster-wise  | 0.345  | 0.188  | 0.332  | 0.145\nL396: Random  | 0.345  | 0.187  | 0.331  | 0.145\nL397: To evaluate their impact on the model’s adaptation performance, we conduct a comparative analysis of these policies on a representative subset of our data. Specifically, we sampled 10 users from each group, representing 20% of the users with small and large interaction histories, respectively.\nL398: As shown in Table cite100†4 , our proposed Global Highest policy achieves the best or competitive performance across both tasks. This indicates that prioritizing samples with high drift scores is a promising direction for user adaptation.\nL399: However, comparable performance of cluster-based strategies implies that the optimal balance between difficulty-driven selection and semantic diversity may vary depending on the specific user profile or task complexity, leaving room for further investigation into hybrid retention mechanisms.\nL400: Table 5: Detailed statistics regarding the number of interactions. Comparison between Small and Large interaction groups across tasks.\nL401: Task  | Group  | N  | Mean # Interactions  | Median  | Range (Min–Max)\nL402: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL403: Abstract Generation  | Small Interactions  | 50  | $528.5\\pm 59.2$  | 515.5  | $447-640$\nL404: Large Interactions  | 50  | $1209.9\\pm 460.7$  | 1055.5  | $828-3330$\nL405: Review Writing  | Small Interactions  | 50  | $315.7\\pm 31.0$  | 309.0  | $268-372$\nL406: Large Interactions  | 50  | $558.3\\pm 173.8$  | 515.5  | $379-1267$\nL407: ## Appendix B Datasets\nL408: \nL409: We conduct our xperiments on LongLaMP (cite71†Kumar et al., 2024 ) benchmark. In this section, we provide a detailed description of the datasets used in our experiments, the preprocessing transformations applied to align them with our experimental setup, and their corresponding descriptive statistics.\nL410: \nL411: ### B.1 Dataset specification\nL412: #### Abstract Generation.\nL413: This task assesses the ability of a model to generate scientific abstracts given paper titles and keywords. The user profile consists of the researcher’s previous publications, which the model must leverage to personalize the academic tone, logical structure, and domain-specific vocabulary unique to each author. We follow the User Setting in LongLaMP, where each sample is structured such that both the profile and the target output belong to the same author.\nL414: To adapt this to our setup, we first sort each researcher’s publication history in reverse chronological order based on the publication year. The history is then partitioned into five sequential periods. Within each period, we designate the most recent 10% of publications as test queries (input: title, output: abstract) and the remaining 90% as the user’s historical profile.\nL415: To ensure a sufficiently long-term interaction history for drift analysis, we filter for users whose total profile size exceeds 500 publications.\nL416: #### Review Writing.\nL417: This task involves drafting a detailed product review from a product’s name and description, alongside a summary of the user’s review. The profile includes a user’s past reviews and ratings, requiring the model to follow the user’s subjective perspective, rating tendencies, and unique writing styles when expressing opinions. Consistent with the User Setting, all historical interactions are mapped to a specific reviewer ID.\nL418: We follow a similar temporal splitting strategy, dividing the history into five chronological periods. For this task, we choose users with a total profile size exceeding 300 reviews to focus on those with rich historical data.\nL419: ### B.2 Dataset statistics\nL420: #### User categorization criteria.\nL421: \nL422: We categorize users based on their total number of historical interactions. To ensure a distinct contrast between the two groups, we utilize the 50th and 80th percentiles of the profile size distribution within each task as thresholds:\nL423: \nL424:   * $\\circ$\nL425: \nL426: Small Interactions: Users within the bottom 50% of the interaction frequency, representing cold-start or low-engagement cases.\nL427: \nL428:   * $\\circ$\nL429: Large Interactions: Users within the top 20% of the interaction frequency, representing users with extensive behavioral data.\nL430: \nL431: We randomly sampled 50 users from each category for our main experiments to maintain computational efficiency.\nL432: #### Descriptive statistics.\nL433: \nL434: Table cite91†5 presents the profile size statistics for each task and group. In Abstract Generation, where only users with more than 500 interactions are included, the Large group exhibits a clearly higher mean profile size, testing the model’s ability to handle long-term shift. In Review Writing, the Small group reflects data sparsity with a minimum of 300 reviews, while we need to deal with a challenge for noise management for the Large group.\nL435: ## Appendix C Baselines\nL436: \nL437: In this section, we provide the implementation details and configurations for the baseline models. To distinguish between the methods, we denote the base model with $\\square$, personalization baselines with $\\boxplus$, and continual learning strategies with $\\blacksquare$. Notably, as the original CL baselines were primarily designed for task-incremental or class-incremental settings, we have adapted their core mechanisms to suit our user-specific, period-wise adaptation setup.\nL438:   * $\\square$\nL439: \nL440: Base is the result of original pre-trained LLM without any additional context or fine-tuning, serving as a zero-shot performance baseline.\nL441: \nL442:   * $\\boxplus$\nL443: RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) (cite64†Hwang et al., 2023 ) is a strategy that retrieves the top-$k$ relevant records from the user’s history as in-context examples. In our setup, we utilize the BM25Okapi implementation from the rank_bm25 library to retrieve the top-4 interactions.\nL444: We consider two variations: RAG each, which restricts the retrieval pool to the history within the same period $t$ as the test query, and RAG accum, which performs retrieval from the cumulative history from periods $0$ to $t$.\nL445:   * $\\boxplus$\nL446: PAG (Profile-Augmented Generation) (cite79†Richardson et al., 2023 ) is a method that utilizes a generated user profile as context, following the profiling strategy of prior work (cite65†Tan et al., 2024 ). For each user, we construct a profile by randomly sampling $k=10$ historical interactions and summarizing them using Gemma-3-4B-IT.\nL447: Similar to RAG, we evaluate two settings: PAG each, where the 10 samples are drawn exclusively from the history within the current period $t$, and PAG accum, where the 10 samples are selected uniformly from the cumulative history across periods $0$ to $t$.\nL448:   * $\\boxplus$\nL449: OPPU (One PEFT per User) (cite65†Tan et al., 2024 ) originally trains individual user LoRA adapters starting from a Task Adapted Model (TAM). In our implementation, we adapt this method by directly training individual LoRA adapters for each user from the base model, bypassing the intermediate TAM stage to focus on direct user-specific optimization.\nL450: We evaluate two training settings: OPPU each, which fine-tunes the adapter only on the history of the current period $t$, and OPPU accum, which utilizes the cumulative history from periods $0$ to $t$ for training.\nL451:   * $\\blacksquare$\nL452: CAMA (Confidence-Aware Memory Augmentation) (cite72†Kim et al., 2024 ) is a replay-based CL strategy that filters new samples according to the model’s self-confidence. At the start of each new period, the model calculates its average confidence level using samples from the previous period. For new incoming data, only those samples that exceed this average confidence threshold are selected for fine-tuning.\nL453: These high-confidence samples are integrated with a subset of past interactions from the replay buffer, ensuring that the adapter focuses on reliable information while maintaining previously established user patterns.\nL454:   * $\\blacksquare$\nL455: SAR (Similarity-Aware Replay) (cite80†Seo et al., 2025 ) optimizes the replay process by maintaining a balanced representation of the user’s behavioral space. This method clusters user interactions in the hidden representation space and tracks a use-frequency score for each sample. To prevent the model from overfitting to repetitive patterns, SAR assigns higher sampling probabilities to interactions that are from under-represented clusters or have been less frequently used in training.\nL456: By accounting for similarity and usage history, it ensures that the replay buffer provides a diverse and comprehensive coverage of the user’s history.\nL457:   * $\\blacksquare$\nL458: O-LoRA (Orthogonal LoRA) (cite81†Wang et al., 2023 ) mitigates catastrophic forgetting by constraining learning updates for different periods to mutually orthogonal subspaces. When training on a new period, the model projects the weight updates onto the orthogonal complement of the space utilized in previous stages.\nL459: This restriction prevents new knowledge from distorting the previously learned weights, allowing the model to accumulate information across multiple periods without degrading performance on earlier tasks.\nL460:   * $\\blacksquare$\nL461: EWC-LoRA (Elastic Weight Consolidation for LoRA) (cite82†Xiang et al., 2023 ) is a method that preserves past knowledge by penalizing changes to parameters that were critical for previous learning stages. After each period, the model estimates the importance of each LoRA parameter based on its contribution to the past data distribution. During the training of a new period, a regularization penalty term is added to the loss function, which restricts the movement of these important parameters.\nL462: This elastic constraint forces the model to learn new information primarily through less critical parameters, thereby consolidating user-specific knowledge acquired in earlier stages.\nL463: ## Appendix D Extended Ablation Studies\nL464: \nL465: ### D.1 Training dynamics: drift score analysis\nL466: #### Structural analysis of drift score components.\nL467: \nL468: We analyze the relationship between the Novelty Term (N) and the Quality Term (Q) to understand the mechanics of our Drift Score (D) (Eq cite85†1 ).\nL469: \nL470:  | $$D=\\underbrace{-\\log\\frac{p_{\\mathrm{adap}}}{p_{\\mathrm{base}}}}_{N}\\;+\\;\\underbrace{\\alpha\\log p_{\\mathrm{base}}}_{Q}$$  |  | (9)\nL471: cite101†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: Correlation between quality and novelty terms. The scatter plots show the relationship between the Quality Term and the Novelty Term. Spearman’s $\\rho$ is $0.6647$ for Abstract Generation and $0.3204$ for Review Writing. Table 6: Robustness to the balancing parameter $\\alpha$. Performance comparison using $\\alpha$ for the drift score.\nL472: Methods  | Abstract Generation  | Review Writing\nL473: --- | --- | ---\nL474: ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L\nL475: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL476: $\\alpha=0$  | 0.324  | 0.154  | 0.314  | 0.125\nL477: $\\alpha=0.5$  | 0.323  | 0.155  | 0.313  | 0.125\nL478: $\\alpha=1.0$ (Ours)  | 0.324  | 0.155  | 0.313  | 0.125\nL479: $\\alpha=\\infty$  | 0.323  | 0.155  | 0.313  | 0.125\nL480: Table 7: Examples of low-quality interactions within the benchmark datasets. These samples are successfully identified and filtered by our drift score safeguard due to pre-existing noise in the source data.\nL481: Task  | Dataset Content\nL482: --- | ---\nL483: Abstract Gen.  | Title: “Power-Performance Tradeoff in a Federated Cloud.”\nL484:  | Abstract: “This installment of Computer’s series highlighting the work published in IEEE Computer Society journals comes from IEEE Transactions on Computers.”\nL485:  | (The text incorrectly contains journal metadata instead of a research abstract.)\nL486: Abstract Gen.  | Title: “Integrative analysis of methylation and transcriptional profiles to predict aging and construct aging specific cross-tissue networks.”\nL487:  | Abstract: “Our results find important coordinated aging patterns between different tissues. Both co-profiling and cross-pathway analyses identify more thorough functions of aging, and could help to find aging markers, pathways and relative aging disease researches.”\nL488:  | (The text provides only a brief summary of results, lacking the structural depth required for a complete research abstract.)\nL489: Review Writing  | Product: “It Started in Naples VHS”,\nL490: Description: “It Started in Naples VHS Tape”,\nL491: Overall rating: “4.0”,\nL492: Summary: “adorable romantic romp with Sophia Loren”\nL493:  | Review: “Yet another charming romantic comedy with Sophia Loren, this time paired with Clark Gable. Gable plays a stuffy Bostonian lawyer, in Naples to meet the young son of his dead brother. He quickly forms an attachment to little Nando (played winningly by Marietto) however his Aunt Lucia (Loren) refuses to let him take the child back to the States. As you might expect however, the charm of Capri soon works it’s magic and the pair find love in the final reel. Sunny funny romance.\nL494: This is probably one of Sophia’s best pictures of the time. As Lucia, a stunning uninhibited cabaret star, she gets to show off her seldom-utilised musical skills performing several numbers including \"(You Wanna Be) Americano\". Though Loren and Gable are somewhat lacking in the chemistry department, the movie as a whole is lovely. The DVD edition of the film offers a pristine 16:9 transfer.”\nL495:  | (The prompt requests a review for a VHS tape, but the text discusses a DVD transfer.)\nL496: Review Writing  | Product: “CENTURY SPRING 4002 Swing Extension Spring (2 Pack)”,\nL497: Description: “2 pack, porch swing extension spring, used primarily for porch swings & will support approximately 300 lbs each, use as tension for fence, dog chain, adjust, tire swings, porch chairs & other uses where safety is a primary concern.”,\nL498: Overall rating: “5.0”,\nL499: Summary: “springing up life on the front porch”\nL500:  | Review: “With these springs you can watch the day go by once again! Sunrise, sunset, neighborhood kids going by on their bikes, dogs barking in your neighbors yard, mischievous situations brewing in the trees amongst the raccoons and squirrels, etc. These springs will actually work on your porch swing really well, and I’m surprised since my knowledge with the subject is limited greatly to just, well, a spring is a spring, lol.\nL501: Not really sure if there’s other types of springs that work more effectively or what. Either way the porch swing works so I don’t have to dirty my bottom using the porch steps and watch my back for ants that may be crawling up my shirt. That makes me very nervous.”\nL502:  | (The text includes irrelevant personal anecdotes that do not align with the functional utility of the product.)\nL503: This analysis is conducted on a representative subset of our data for both tasks. Specifically, we sampled 10 users from each group, which are 20% of the users with small and large interactions, respectively. Figure cite102†4 shows that a positive correlation exists between $Q$ and $N$ across all period ($r=0.66$ for Abstract Generation and $r=0.32$ for Review Writing).\nL504: This diagonal distribution indicates that high-quality samples which are linguistically well-formed tend to naturally align with high-novelty signals. The relatively vacant top-left quadrant (low quality, high novelty) suggests that our benchmark datasets contain minimal pseudo-novelty from gibberish or noise.\nL505: This qualitative analysis confirms that instances involving irrelevant system metadata or extremely short text lengths are relegated to the bottom-left quadrant, as they lack both linguistic coherence and informative value for learning (Table  cite103†7 ).\nL506: #### Robustness to balancing parameter $\\alpha$.\nL507: The structural coupling between $Q$ and $N$ in the high-fidelity region explains the empirical robustness of our method to the choice of the balancing weight $\\alpha$. As shown in Table cite104†6 , varying $\\alpha$ from 0.0 to $\\infty$ yields nearly identical performance in both ROUGE-1 and ROUGE-L scores across tasks.\nL508: Since high-quality and high-novelty samples largely overlap in the top-right region of the distribution, the same group of informative samples is consistently chosen as drift samples regardless of the balancing weight $\\alpha$. Nevertheless, we maintain $\\alpha=1.0$ as a precautionary safeguard in practical deployments to explicitly penalize potental noise and ensure that only linguistically coherent signals are selected for adaptation.\nL509: #### Distinguishing drift score from hard example mining.\nL510: \nL511: A potential concern is whether our drift score simply performs conventional hard example mining by targeting samples with low $p_{\\texttt{adap}}$. To clarify the distinction, we define the Hardness Term ($H$) of a sample as the negative log-likelihood under the adapted model.\nL512: \nL513:  | $$H=-\\log p_{\\texttt{adap}}$$  |  | (10)\nL514: \nL515: Using Eq. cite105†9 , the hardness term can be written as\nL516: \n"
